<!--
    `iteexpr` (option, default-off): extend the `iteregion` diamond -> `?:` ternary
    rewrite from single-COPY arms to any single-statement COMPUTED pure-value arm.

    angr's ITERegionConverter folds `if (c) v = <expr1>; else v = <expr2>;` into
    `v = c ? expr1 : expr2` for ANY pure-value expression arm (a LOAD, an INT_ADD,
    ...), not just a plain COPY.  kuna's shipped `iteregion` (DIV-17, default-on)
    only matched single-COPY arms, so it left the far more common computed-arm
    diamonds as if/else - on decbench O0 coreutils angr emits ~1389 ternaries to
    kuna's ~177.  `iteexpr` on broadens the arm match (`single_assign_arm`) to any
    single pure-value op (rejecting only side-effecting/control ops), roughly
    doubling kuna's ternary recovery.  Print-only, and because a C ternary evaluates
    only the taken branch it is semantics-preserving.

    Reproducer (gcc -O0, expr_arm):
        long expr_arm(long a, long b) { long x; if (a) x = b + 5; else x = b - 3;
                                        return x; }
    Each arm is a single COMPUTED assignment (`x = b + 5` / `x = b - 3`) is an
    INT_ADD/INT_SUB, not a COPY, so shipped iteregion declines it.

    Pass 1 (option iteexpr off, the DEFAULT): iteregion (on) sees only COPY arms, so
    the computed-arm diamond stays an `if (a != 0) { x = b + 5; } else { x = b - 3; }`
    with NO ternary.
    Pass 2 (option iteexpr on): the arm match broadens to the INT_ADD/INT_SUB arms,
    so the diamond folds to `x = ( a != 0 ) ? b + 5 : b - 3;` giving exactly one `?:`.
-->
